</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Results</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="A4.SS1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D.1</span> </span><span id="A4.SS1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experiment 1</span></h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experiment 1</span> assessed the effects of prompt language (English vs. Chinese), LLM origin (Chinese vs. English), and framing (neutral vs. change-framed) on model performance to rate for the tasks of cultural stability and entity change across 960 observations, using paired and independent <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests, Wilcoxon tests, and a three-way ANOVA.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results are summarized in Table <a href="#A4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ D.1 Experiment 1 ‣ Appendix D Results ‣ The fragility of “cultural tendencies” in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. A smaller but statistically significant main effect was observed for <span id="A4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM origin</span>: Chinese LLMs outperformed English LLMs by <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.021" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.021</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.021</annotation></semantics></math> points overall (<math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.0089" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0089</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.0089</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=0.169" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.169</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=0.169</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}=0.0071" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0071</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}=0.0071</annotation></semantics></math>). This advantage was most evident for Chinese-prompt inputs (difference <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.0283" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0283</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.0283</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.0144" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0144</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.0144</annotation></semantics></math>), while the English-prompt condition showed no significant origin effect (<math id="A4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.219" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.219</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.219</annotation></semantics></math>), suggesting a mild cultural–linguistic alignment benefit.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, <span id="A4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">prompt language</span> showed no statistically detectable influence on performance overall (mean difference <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.0053" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0053</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.0053</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.1501" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1501</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.1501</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=0.045" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.045</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=0.045</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}=0.0002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}=0.0002</annotation></semantics></math>), and this null effect held within both Chinese LLMs (<math id="A4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.0210" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0210</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.0210</annotation></semantics></math> with a very small effect) and English LLMs (<math id="A4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.4432" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4432</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.4432</annotation></semantics></math>). While two models (DeepSeek, Qwen) showed significant language preferences, the effect was inconsistent in direction and negligible in magnitude for the others, indicating largely language-agnostic performance at the group level.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, the other statistical effects are summarized in Table <a href="#A4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ D.1 Experiment 1 ‣ Appendix D Results ‣ The fragility of “cultural tendencies” in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. As shown in Table <a href="#A4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ D.1 Experiment 1 ‣ Appendix D Results ‣ The fragility of “cultural tendencies” in LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> the clearest and largest effect was <span id="A4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">framing</span> (vs. neutral). Change-framed prompts yielded markedly higher scores than neutral prompts (mean difference <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=-0.1650" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.1650</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=-0.1650</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;2.2\times 10^{-16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;2.2\times 10^{-16}</annotation></semantics></math>, Cohen’s <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=-2.095" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.095</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=-2.095</annotation></semantics></math>), an effect confirmed as the dominant source of variance in the ANOVA (<math id="A4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}=0.5423" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5423</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}=0.5423</annotation></semantics></math>), explaining more than half of the total performance variability. This framing advantage was highly robust, appearing across all statistical approaches and for all eight models tested. the ANOVA detected a significant interaction between LLM origin and framing (<math id="A4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1.28\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.28</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1.28\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}=0.0083" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0083</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}=0.0083</annotation></semantics></math>): both Chinese and English LLMs benefited from change-framing, but the extent of the boost differed by origin, pointing to possible differences in framing sensitivity across model families.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T5" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>The main results for Experiment 1. (Model-specific language effects (Chinese vs. English prompts)</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
