<p id="A4.T5.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:293.3pt;"><span id="A4.T5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Note.<span id="A4.T5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Prompt language influenced scores in 2/8 models (i.e., <span id="A4.T5.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">qwen</span>, <span id="A4.T5.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">deepseek</span>, 25%), with effects limited to Chinese models. However, across all models, prompt language (English vs. Chinese) did not significantly influence performance (<math id="A4.T5.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.1501" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.1501</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.1501</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.T5.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}=0.0002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}=0.0002</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating negligible practical effect.</span></span></p></div></div>
</figure>
